1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,725
AI can write code in minutes.

2
00:00:02,725 --> 00:00:07,070
But an enterprise needs a system it trusts before it can go into production.

3
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:14,635
A production system is complete, tested, and adheres to its corporate privacy, security, architecture and risk standards.

4
00:00:16,185 --> 00:00:21,565
Akka Specify creates and runs software factories for your IT initiatives.

5
00:00:21,565 --> 00:00:30,870
Each factory builds its system end to end: it writes the code, runs the system, and validates it against every corporate policy.

6
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:41,425
You make decisions that only people with an understanding of your business should make: what the system is for, how to deal with ambiguity, and what must pass before a release can be made.

7
00:00:42,975 --> 00:00:47,940
Four things make systems created with Akka Specify production-ready.

8
00:00:47,940 --> 00:00:54,530
Every fact a factory uses to design a system is traced and recorded in a reusable specification.

9
00:00:54,530 --> 00:00:59,100
Every behaviour is checked against the original system, if one exists.

10
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:05,570
Every review the enterprise requires is an exit condition, checked automatically by auditors.

11
00:01:05,570 --> 00:01:11,435
And it runs on Akka's platform, inside your own network, with Akka's guarantees for resilience and scalability.

12
00:01:12,985 --> 00:01:17,850
Akka has delivered factories that work in multiple regulated environments.

13
00:01:17,850 --> 00:01:24,815
At a regulated payments provider, recent college graduates put an AI system into production in weeks.

14
00:01:24,815 --> 00:01:32,520
A global insurer rebuilt a twenty-year-old underwriting calculator in under a week, matching the original case for case.

15
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:38,215
A Fortune 200 financial services firm moved its payments off a forty-year-old mainframe in a couple of months.

16
00:01:39,765 --> 00:01:44,940
A factory can start from an idea or from a system that already exists.

17
00:01:44,940 --> 00:01:55,875
From an idea, discovery turns user requirements, support history and an analysis of peers and rivals into the specification of a new system, an AI system or any other.

18
00:01:55,875 --> 00:02:06,690
From an existing system, the factory ports or modernizes it, and the original becomes the framework for validating and proving that the new system is a behavioral match.

19
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:10,645
Both paths end in the same place: a tested system ready for production.

20
00:02:12,195 --> 00:02:21,770
Let's see how Akka Specify works by following the modernization of an existing system, from what it does today to a verified twin in pre-production.

21
00:02:21,770 --> 00:02:28,255
The factory has five stations: discover, decide, specify, build and release.

22
00:02:29,805 --> 00:02:32,250
The system is a freight-rating engine.

23
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:37,755
It prices every shipment a logistics company carries, and it is twenty-two years old.

24
00:02:37,755 --> 00:02:44,770
It lives in a tariff book, in the code around it and in a production service, and what it does is known only by running it.

25
00:02:44,770 --> 00:02:50,195
We'll do this within Claude Code or your favorite IDE or harness with Akka's AI plugins added.

26
00:02:51,745 --> 00:02:56,720
Discovery gathers everything that tells the factory what the system should do.

27
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:07,470
It reads the user requirements, the support tickets of past years, and an analysis of peers and rivals, which suggests capabilities that make sense for this enterprise.

28
00:03:07,470 --> 00:03:10,995
When a system already exists, discovery reads it and runs it too.

29
00:03:12,545 --> 00:03:19,260
For the freight-rating engine, the sources are the tariff book, the code, production behaviour and the people who run it.

30
00:03:19,260 --> 00:03:23,625
Each fact discovery finds is written down with the evidence behind it.

31
00:03:23,625 --> 00:03:29,560
A person confirms each fact before anything is built on it, so the rebuild rests only on what is known.

32
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:35,515
A probe is a check that runs the original system and records what it does.

33
00:03:35,515 --> 00:03:47,765
Akka Specify ships with numerous probes to identify routes and data, the services it calls, and how it behaves over a sequence of calls, over time, and across the range of an input.

34
00:03:47,765 --> 00:03:54,530
A fact a probe exercised is stronger than a fact read in the code, and the record says which kind each fact is.

35
00:03:56,080 --> 00:04:02,175
Analysis lines the sources up and lists every place where ambiguity or a conflict appears.

36
00:04:02,175 --> 00:04:06,800
Here, the tariff book and production apply two surcharges in a different order.

37
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:13,595
Each conflict becomes a decision that an owner signs before any code is written, and every difference that remains is recorded.

38
00:04:15,145 --> 00:04:18,110
The original system becomes the answer key.

39
00:04:18,110 --> 00:04:25,645
Shipments are generated at every weight break, every zone edge and every conflict, and the original prices each one.

40
00:04:25,645 --> 00:04:31,100
Parity means the rebuild gives the same price, to the cent, for every one of them.

41
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:34,265
The rebuild's correctness is then measured, shipment by shipment.

42
00:04:35,815 --> 00:04:48,320
The specification holds what the system is for: its user stories, its requirements, its measurable success criteria, the owner's decisions and the exit conditions it must pass.

43
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:52,815
An exit condition is a rule a release must meet before it ships.

44
00:04:52,815 --> 00:04:59,095
Every requirement cites the finding it rests on, so every line of the specification rests on evidence.

45
00:05:00,645 --> 00:05:05,950
The plan breaks the specification into tasks and works out which can run at the same time.

46
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:10,305
Tasks that can run together do, across user stories.

47
00:05:10,305 --> 00:05:17,770
Each task is matched to a model by risk: the rating rules go to the most capable model, routine components to a faster one.

48
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:33,905
The factory designs this system from Akka's components, chosen and composed for what it must do: endpoints for its callers, a workflow, event-sourced entities that keep every change, and views for queries.

49
00:05:33,905 --> 00:05:38,490
The rating rules stay in plain code that can be read and tested on its own.

50
00:05:38,490 --> 00:05:41,230
Akka's components combine into any enterprise system.

51
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:55,805
The factory also writes this system's documentation: its component graph, its domain model, its state lifecycles, an interaction diagram for every flow, and a reference for every API route.

52
00:05:57,355 --> 00:06:00,210
Tests are written before the code they test.

53
00:06:00,210 --> 00:06:06,590
Integration tests run the components on the real runtime, and the answer key runs on every build.

54
00:06:06,590 --> 00:06:12,985
If a price drifts from the original, the build fails at that moment, months before acceptance testing.

55
00:06:12,985 --> 00:06:15,600
On every build, the rebuild and the original agree.

56
00:06:17,150 --> 00:06:23,365
An organization sets its exit conditions once, and they apply to every project's factory.

57
00:06:23,365 --> 00:06:30,990
They say what privacy, security, architecture and risk require of every system the organization releases.

58
00:06:30,990 --> 00:06:34,585
An auditor is the automatic check behind each condition.

59
00:06:34,585 --> 00:06:38,750
It runs a command, probes the running service or reads the code.

60
00:06:38,750 --> 00:06:43,785
A system that passes them has passed the reviews the organization defined, before the reviewers meet.

61
00:06:45,335 --> 00:06:49,260
Every requirement is traced to a task and to a test.

62
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:54,900
Every decision, every recorded difference and every model call is kept with the change.

63
00:06:54,900 --> 00:06:57,425
The reviewers read one record, and it is complete.

64
00:06:58,975 --> 00:07:03,175
Akka's platform runs inside the organization's own network.

65
00:07:03,175 --> 00:07:14,330
A release that passes its exit conditions goes straight into pre-production for user acceptance, already active in two regions, with high availability and disaster recovery.

66
00:07:14,330 --> 00:07:19,645
The original keeps pricing shipments beside it until the parallel run shows they agree on live traffic.

67
00:07:21,195 --> 00:07:28,690
Specify also runs factories at scale: each project runs as its own factory, and many run at once.

68
00:07:28,690 --> 00:07:33,030
Akka ported sixty-five open-source projects at once this way.

69
00:07:33,030 --> 00:07:38,445
Specify assesses each factory's performance, measures its cost and improves the next iteration.

70
00:07:39,995 --> 00:07:45,400
Enterprises come to Akka for an AI platform, and put an innovation engine to work.

71
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:47,915
Modernization is one use of it.

72
00:07:47,915 --> 00:07:53,050
Each idea gets a factory of its own, whether it is an AI system or any other IT system.

73
00:07:54,600 --> 00:08:04,095
Every idea on an organization's roadmap can have its own factory: a new AI system, a new service, or a system that already exists.

74
00:08:04,095 --> 00:08:07,880
Specify runs as many factories as there are projects.

75
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:14,825
The roadmap collapses, and the innovation planned for 2028 and 2029 can be delivered in 2027.

76
00:08:16,375 --> 00:08:27,140
Running a project's factory takes a product owner's judgement: knowing what the system is for, deciding where the sources disagree, and approving what it must pass.

77
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:31,875
What each system does is kept in its specification, where anyone who joins can read it.

78
00:08:33,425 --> 00:08:38,520
Bring one idea from your roadmap, or one system you would like to modernize.

79
00:08:38,520 --> 00:08:45,175
We will run it through its own factory with you and show you its specification, its plan and its exit conditions.
